h1. F5 DCP PROD/DR Failover Reference

h2. Purpose

This document records the F5 configuration used for the DCP application, including the previous configuration, the updated configuration, and the expected PROD/DR failover behavior.

The application uses an F5 Virtual Server with URI-based routing to three DCP application pools:

* /dcp01 → DCP01 Pool
* /dcp02 → DCP02 Pool
* /dcp03 → DCP03 Pool

The same logical configuration exists in both the PROD and DR environments.

The purpose of this document is to provide a future reference for:

* The original F5 configuration.
* The reason the Default Pool configuration was changed.
* The current F5 configuration.
* How DCP01, DCP02 and DCP03 health affects PROD/DR failover.
* Operational considerations associated with using DCP01 as the Default Pool.

⸻

h1. 1. Previous Configuration

Previously, the F5 Virtual Server did not have a Default Pool configured.

URI-based F5 policies or rules were responsible for routing requests to the appropriate DCP pool.

{code}
Application VIP
|
F5 Virtual Server
Default Pool: None
|
+———––+———––+
|             |             |
/dcp01        /dcp02        /dcp03
|             |             |
DCP01 Pool     DCP02 Pool     DCP03 Pool
{code}

h3. Previous Configuration Summary

|| URI / Setting || Target Pool / Value ||
| /dcp01 | DCP01 Pool |
| /dcp02 | DCP02 Pool |
| /dcp03 | DCP03 Pool |
| Default Pool | None |
| URI Routing | Enabled |

h3. Previous Request Routing

The routing behavior was:

{code}
/dcp01  –>  DCP01 Pool
/dcp02  –>  DCP02 Pool
/dcp03  –>  DCP03 Pool
{code}

There was no generic backend pool configured directly against the F5 Virtual Server.

⸻

h1. 2. Observed Behavior with No Default Pool

During PROD/DR active-active operation, automatic failover was not working as expected.

Although DCP01, DCP02 and DCP03 had their own backend pool health monitoring, the Virtual Server itself did not have a Default Pool associated with it.

The configuration therefore looked logically like:

{code}
Application VIP
|
Virtual Server
|
Default Pool = None
|
URI Routing
|
+———+———+———+
|         |         |
DCP01     DCP02     DCP03
Pool      Pool      Pool
{code}

When backend application servers became unavailable, the required Virtual Server availability state was not being reflected correctly for the existing PROD/DR automatic failover mechanism.

This resulted in a situation where the individual backend pools could be unhealthy, while the overall PROD/DR failover behavior did not occur as expected.

⸻

h1. 3. Configuration Change

To address the failover behavior, the F5 configuration was updated so that DCP01 Pool is configured as the Default Pool for the Virtual Server.

The existing URI-based routing remains unchanged.

{code}
Application VIP
|
F5 Virtual Server
Default Pool: DCP01
|
+———––+———––+
|             |             |
/dcp01        /dcp02        /dcp03
|             |             |
DCP01 Pool     DCP02 Pool     DCP03 Pool
{code}

h3. Current Configuration Summary

|| URI / Setting || Target Pool / Value ||
| /dcp01 | DCP01 Pool |
| /dcp02 | DCP02 Pool |
| /dcp03 | DCP03 Pool |
| Default Pool | DCP01 Pool |
| URI Routing | Enabled |

The request routing remains:

{code}
/dcp01  –>  DCP01 Pool
/dcp02  –>  DCP02 Pool
/dcp03  –>  DCP03 Pool
{code}

DCP01 now additionally serves as the Default Pool for the F5 Virtual Server.

⸻

h1. 4. Before vs. After

|| Configuration || Before || After ||
| /dcp01 routing | DCP01 Pool | DCP01 Pool |
| /dcp02 routing | DCP02 Pool | DCP02 Pool |
| /dcp03 routing | DCP03 Pool | DCP03 Pool |
| Default Pool | None | DCP01 Pool |
| URI routing logic | Enabled | Enabled |
| DCP01 URI behavior | DCP01 Pool | DCP01 Pool |
| DCP02 URI behavior | DCP02 Pool | DCP02 Pool |
| DCP03 URI behavior | DCP03 Pool | DCP03 Pool |
| PROD/DR automatic failover | Not working as expected | Working with current configuration |

⸻

h1. 5. Reason for the Change

The change was introduced because the previous configuration did not have a Default Pool associated with the F5 Virtual Server.

In the previous setup:

{code}
Default Pool = None
{code}

the existing automatic PROD/DR failover mechanism was not receiving the required Virtual Server availability state.

DCP01 was selected as the Default Pool so that the Virtual Server has a defined backend pool whose health participates in the current availability and failover behavior.

No changes were made to the existing URI routing for DCP01, DCP02 or DCP03.

The current configuration is therefore:

{code}
Default Pool = DCP01

URI Routing:
/dcp01 –> DCP01
/dcp02 –> DCP02
/dcp03 –> DCP03
{code}

⸻

h1. 6. Failover Behavior with DCP01 as Default Pool

With DCP01 configured as the Default Pool, DCP01 health becomes important to the overall F5 Virtual Server availability.

The current routing model is:

{code}
/dcp01 –> DCP01 Pool
/dcp02 –> DCP02 Pool
/dcp03 –> DCP03 Pool

Default Pool –> DCP01 Pool
{code}

The following sections document the expected behavior for different backend failure scenarios.

⸻

h2. Scenario 1 - All DCP Pools Are Healthy

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = UP
DCP02 = UP
DCP03 = UP
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

* DCP01 Default Pool remains healthy.
* PROD VIP remains available.
* /dcp01 traffic is routed to DCP01.
* /dcp02 traffic is routed to DCP02.
* /dcp03 traffic is routed to DCP03.
* No PROD to DR failover occurs.

h3. Flow

{code}
DCP01 = UP
DCP02 = UP
DCP03 = UP
|
v
Default Pool DCP01 = UP
|
v
PROD VIP = AVAILABLE
|
v
No Failover
{code}

⸻

h2. Scenario 2 - DCP01 Servers Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = DOWN
DCP02 = UP
DCP03 = UP
{code}

Because DCP01 is configured as the Default Pool, its health participates in determining the availability of the F5 Virtual Server.

h3. Expected Behavior

* All healthy members behind the DCP01 pool are unavailable.
* DCP01 Default Pool becomes unavailable.
* The PROD Virtual Server becomes unavailable for the existing failover decision.
* PROD to DR automatic failover is triggered.
* Traffic is moved to the DR environment according to the existing F5 PROD/DR configuration.

h3. Flow

{code}
DCP01 Servers DOWN
|
v
DCP01 Pool DOWN
|
v
Default Pool DOWN
|
v
PROD VIP becomes unavailable
|
v
PROD –> DR Failover
{code}

h3. Important Behavior

Even if DCP02 and DCP03 in PROD are still healthy:

{code}
DCP01 = DOWN
DCP02 = UP
DCP03 = UP
{code}

the overall PROD VIP can fail over because DCP01 is the Default Pool.

This means DCP01 health is currently significant to the overall site-level failover behavior.

⸻

h2. Scenario 3 - DCP02 Servers Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = UP
DCP02 = DOWN
DCP03 = UP
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

* DCP01 Default Pool remains healthy.
* PROD VIP remains available.
* PROD to DR site-level failover is not triggered solely because DCP02 is down.
* /dcp01 continues to operate normally.
* /dcp03 continues to operate normally.
* Requests to /dcp02 are impacted because the DCP02 pool has no healthy backend servers.

h3. Flow

{code}
DCP02 Servers DOWN
|
v
DCP02 Pool DOWN
|
v
DCP01 Default Pool still UP
|
v
PROD VIP remains AVAILABLE
|
v
No site-level failover
{code}

h3. Result

The failure is primarily limited to requests targeting:

{code}
/dcp02
{code}

while DCP01 and DCP03 can continue serving traffic.

⸻

h2. Scenario 4 - DCP03 Servers Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = UP
DCP02 = UP
DCP03 = DOWN
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

* DCP01 Default Pool remains healthy.
* PROD VIP remains available.
* PROD to DR site-level failover is not triggered solely because DCP03 is down.
* /dcp01 continues to operate.
* /dcp02 continues to operate.
* Requests to /dcp03 are impacted because the DCP03 pool has no healthy backend servers.

h3. Flow

{code}
DCP03 Servers DOWN
|
v
DCP03 Pool DOWN
|
v
DCP01 Default Pool still UP
|
v
PROD VIP remains AVAILABLE
|
v
No site-level failover
{code}

h3. Result

The failure is primarily limited to requests targeting:

{code}
/dcp03
{code}

while DCP01 and DCP02 continue serving traffic.

⸻

h2. Scenario 5 - DCP02 and DCP03 Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = UP
DCP02 = DOWN
DCP03 = DOWN
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

* DCP01 remains healthy.
* DCP01 Default Pool remains available.
* PROD VIP remains available.
* PROD to DR site-level failover is not triggered based only on DCP02 and DCP03 failures.
* /dcp01 continues to operate.
* /dcp02 requests are impacted.
* /dcp03 requests are impacted.

h3. Flow

{code}
DCP02 DOWN
DCP03 DOWN
|
v
DCP01 Default Pool still UP
|
v
PROD VIP remains AVAILABLE
|
v
No overall PROD –> DR failover
{code}

This is an important behavior of the current configuration.

Even though two application pools may be unavailable, the overall VIP can remain available as long as the DCP01 Default Pool remains healthy.

⸻

h2. Scenario 6 - DCP01 and DCP02 Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = DOWN
DCP02 = DOWN
DCP03 = UP
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

Because DCP01 is the Default Pool:

{code}
DCP01 DOWN
|
v
Default Pool DOWN
|
v
PROD VIP unavailable
|
v
PROD –> DR Failover
{code}

The availability of DCP03 does not prevent the overall failover if the current failover decision is based on DCP01 Default Pool health.

⸻

h2. Scenario 7 - DCP01 and DCP03 Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = DOWN
DCP02 = UP
DCP03 = DOWN
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

Because DCP01 is the Default Pool:

{code}
DCP01 DOWN
|
v
Default Pool DOWN
|
v
PROD VIP unavailable
|
v
PROD –> DR Failover
{code}

DCP02 being healthy does not prevent the overall failover when DCP01 is unavailable.

⸻

h2. Scenario 8 - All DCP Pools Are Down

h3. Pool Status

{code}
DCP01 = DOWN
DCP02 = DOWN
DCP03 = DOWN
{code}

h3. Expected Behavior

* DCP01 Default Pool is unavailable.
* PROD VIP becomes unavailable.
* Automatic PROD to DR failover occurs, assuming the corresponding DR environment is healthy.

h3. Flow

{code}
DCP01 DOWN
DCP02 DOWN
DCP03 DOWN
|
v
DCP01 Default Pool DOWN
|
v
PROD VIP DOWN
|
v
PROD –> DR Failover
{code}

⸻

h1. 7. Failover Summary

|| DCP01 || DCP02 || DCP03 || VIP / Site Status || Expected PROD to DR Failover ||
| UP | UP | UP | UP | No |
| DOWN | UP | UP | Unavailable | Yes |
| UP | DOWN | UP | UP | No |
| UP | UP | DOWN | UP | No |
| UP | DOWN | DOWN | UP | No |
| DOWN | DOWN | UP | Unavailable | Yes |
| DOWN | UP | DOWN | Unavailable | Yes |
| DOWN | DOWN | DOWN | Unavailable | Yes |

⸻

h1. 8. Key Failover Principle

Under the current configuration:

{code}
DCP01 failure
|
v
Default Pool failure
|
v
PROD VIP unavailable
|
v
PROD –> DR failover
{code}

Whereas:

{code}
DCP02 or DCP03 failure
|
v
DCP01 remains healthy
|
v
Default Pool remains healthy
|
v
PROD VIP remains available
|
v
No overall PROD –> DR failover
{code}

Therefore, the current configuration effectively provides:

Site-level failover primarily based on DCP01 Default Pool health.

It does not provide independent PROD/DR failover based on the health of every individual DCP URI pool.

⸻

h1. 9. Operational Considerations

With the current configuration, DCP01 is significant to the overall VIP health and failover behavior because it is configured as the Default Pool.

Any future change to the following DCP01 components should consider the possible impact on PROD/DR failover:

* DCP01 backend servers.
* DCP01 pool members.
* DCP01 health monitor.
* DCP01 application health endpoint.
* DCP01 network connectivity.
* DCP01 maintenance activity.
* DCP01 pool configuration.
* F5 monitor timeout or interval configuration.

A planned DCP01 outage could potentially cause the entire application VIP to fail over to DR even when DCP02 and DCP03 remain healthy.

